Table 12.1 The actions associated with pressing various keys when focus is in the full image viewer window.
The full image viewer window, when it has focus, will respond to keyboard input as shown in table 12.1. This shows that you can navigate to various tiles in the preview (and simultaneously see the selection in the full image viewer window) with intuitive key strokes, making it easy to navigate the scan.
Note that for a scan with a Z component, when you change the Z level of the full image being viewed by means of the 'a' or 'z' keys, that newly selected Z level will pertain to all other images you see as you move around in X and Y with the arrow keys but it will not automatically update the Z level of the preview image displayed in the main window. To change that preview image Z level you must use the Z spin box control in the main window as discussed previously. 

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151">Weights</span>

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">File format and origins of a weights file</span>
As introduced on p.51, a scan may have an accompanying weights file. A weights file is a plan text file and its name should have the extension '.xyw' because that is what the file chooser here will filter for. To be loaded and interpeted properly by the pardclient parser, a weights file must obey the following formatting rules.
The file must contains a list of floating point numbers in ASCII format with each number on a separate line. The newline is the only delimiter that must be used. The numbers must not be enclosed in quote marks of any kind and must not have commas or other delimiter characters.
Blank lines will be ignored by the parser. They are allowed and will not cause any problems.
Lines beginning with a hash character, '#', are also ignored so you can use the hash to enter comment lines in the weights file.
There must be one floating point number entry line for every tile in the scan these weights pertain to. The weights numbers must occur in the exact same order as the images for the scan are listed in the scan file. For 'images' that are marked as 'NoTissue' in the scan file the weights file should show a value of -1.0. Otherwise all weights must be &gt;= 0.0. There is no upper value limit but all values must be valid float numbers (not 'NaN' or other error code).
A weights file that has no contrast in the weights number values will be rejected (so if every non -1.0 weight entry has a value of 0.5, for example, there is no contrast and that file will be rejected if you try to load it).
How you create a weights file is entirely up to you. You may create it during a scan with the custom image process facility allowed for scans (p.50) or you may create it after a scan using some custom post processing on all the images in the scan. As long as the weights file follows the format specified above it should be acceptable to use as a weights file for its accompanying scan for the purposes of loading it into the scan viewer.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">Loading a weights file</span>
You may only load a weights file if a scan is already loaded. The weights file you load must be a match for the scan that is currently loaded i.e. it must have as many weights entries as there are tiles in the currently loaded scan.
To load the weights file simply drag-drop the .xyw file onto this page or click the 'Load Weights' button to bring up a file chooser and select your weights file. As soon as you complete this process the chosen file will be read, checked for correct syntax and number and type of weights and, if all those checks pass, the weights values will be scaled to values between 0 and 1. This is a linear scaling based on the actual minimum and maximum values of the weights read from the file - the minimum will be mapped to 0 and the maximum to 1 and everything in between will be scaled proportionately into that 0-1 range. This scaling is done inside pardclient and will not alter the actual values recorded in the weights files. Once the scaling is done, the weights will be rendered over the image.
The rendering will make those tiles that are associated with lower weights darker (with any tiles associated with weight 0 being totally black) and those files associated with higher weights less dark (with those tiles associated with a weight of 1.0 being of normal brightness). Thus the tiles in the preview will be displayed over a range from normal brightness to black depending on their weight. An example of a weights rendering is shown in figure 12.5. Note that if you had any tile selected before loading a weights file, that selection will be cleared just prior to rendering. You may continue to select tiles as normal after the weights have been rendered.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">Examining weights</span>
As you select each tile in the preview, the actual (scaled) weight value associated with that tile will be displayed in the read-only entry box on the lower right labelled 'Weight'.
The illustration in figure 12.5 is a very simple example of weights rendering in a very small scan (size 3x2 tiles). The real utility of the weights facility in practice can be seen in a much higher resolution whole slide scan of the granulomatous lymph node stained with the ZN stain to pick out mycobacteria (for diagnosis of TB) introduced in figure 12.3. In the general case for non-immunocompromised patients, ZN positive bacilli are usually very sparse and may take a long time of dedicated screening to pick them out and they may be so sparse as to be missed by even a temporary lapse in concentration of the screener.


[IMAGE:image28.png]

Figure 12.5 A weights file is loaded and rendered. The tile top left has a weight of 0.0 so is shown totally black. The tile bottom left has a weight of 1.0 so is shown with its normal brightness. All tiles in between have a gradation of weights and hence are displayed with a gradation of shading according to their weight.
This example lymph node scan is shown again in figure 12.6 with its associated weights file rendering. In this case the images were processed with a machine vision algorithm that outputs the likelihood of a positive TB bacillus in each image. This likelihood number is the weight. The higher the weight, the greater the likelihood of a diagnostic bacillus in the field.
You can see that the weights file rendering immediately draws the attention of the screener to a small number of very 'bright' hotspots in the scan and the slide screener can concentrate examination for bacilli there while their concentration is fresh and they are not tired. Only if those image fields lack bacilli should the whole slide be screened as normal.

[IMAGE:image29.png]

Figure 12.6 Example of using the weights file render to assist in medical diagnosis of rare events. In this case the weights rendered view (right) correlate with the likelihood of the presence of AAFB (tuberculosis bacilli) in this scan of a ZN-stained lymph node. See text for details. This also indicates the effect of the 'Toggle Weights' button

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">Toggling the weights display</span>
Because the weights rendering partially obscures the underlying scan, there is a quick way to toggle between the plain scan preview render and the weighted render by simply pressing the 'Toggle Weights' button (figure 12.5 and 12.6).

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">Ejecting the weights</span>
The currently loaded weights file will be ejected if the user ejects the main scan (with the 'EJECT XYZ Scan' button).
Although there is no 'Eject Weights' button to eject only the currently loaded weights file, leaving the underlying scan loaded, you can achieve this exact effect by simply attempting to load a weights file that is not properly formatted or has the wrong number of weights (not equal to the number of tiles in the scan). In this case the currently loaded weights will be ejected by internal processes of pardclient in preparation for loading the new weights but, because the new weights will fail the checks, they will not be loaded. The ultimate effect is the ejection of the existing weights leaving the underlying scan intact.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151">Hardware syncing</span>
If the user is logged in to a pardserver and connected to the XYZ microscope stage and camera there are some added features of the Scan Viewer available to them.
For these hardware features to be of maximal benefit it is highly recommended that the currently attached camera is the same as the camera that captured the scan (or at least, functionally compatible with the scan capture camera) and the stage is the same as the stage that was used to capture the scan (or at least be of the same specifications as the stage used to capture the scan). Attempting the following hardware actions on a different or incompatible camera or a stage with different specifications to the scan capture stage may result in unexpected,undefined and/or unwanted behaviour or errors.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">Syncing to the camera</span>
The user may automatically set the image save resolution of the attached camera to the resolution used to take the scan images as recorded in the scan file by pressing the 'Sync. Camera' button.
Only the save resolution of the camera is synced in this operation because there is no foolproof way of automatically syncing all other features of the camera setup used during the scan. For example, if a flat field correction image was used during the scan, that same correction image may no longer be appropriate because the illumination pattern and number and position of any dust specs along the imaging train may have changed in the time between when the scan was first taken and now.
Thus, if full camera synchronisation is required the remaining steps will need to be taken, as appropriate, by the user. A list of all camera settings used during the scan will be present in the scan file to act as a reference and guide to the user for that purpose.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">Syncing to the stage</span>
In order to use the 'Go To XYZ' feature described later, the stage must be set up with the same microstep size, calibration features and other mechanical parameters as were in effect at the time the scan was made. This can be done automatically by clicking the 'Sync. Stage' button.
This will read the following parameters from the scan file and attempt to set them the same on the currently connected XYZ stage:
Maximum microstep denominator
Current microstep denominator
Direction 1 value for X (1 or 0)
Direction 2 value for X (1 or 0)
Direction 1 value for Y (1 or 0)
Direction 2 value for Y (1 or 0)
Units name for X (it is assumed that Y and Z will be using the same units so these will not be checked or synced separately).
Units used (units type) for steppers
Calibration factor at the maximum microstep denominator for X
Calibration factor at the maximum microstep denominator for Y
Calibration factor at the maximum microstep denominator for Z
Acceleration profile (act_accel parameters) for steppers
In addition, for a stage to be considered 'synced', the X and Y axes must be homed to the same limits as they were homed when the scan was performed. However, if the syncing function detects either of these axes are not homed (or homed to a different limit than was used during the scan) it will not attempt to automatically home the axis because homing may require certain manual interventions (like removing the slide to prevent damage). Instead it will pop up a message informing you that you need to do it. A 'fully synced' signal will not be set in the program unless such homing is performed and confirmed. So if, you get this message, home the required axis / axes and then click the 'Sync. Stage' button again to re-assess sync status.
Only if all the above items are successfully synced with the stage be marked as 'fully synced' with the current scan and only then will you be allowed to use the 'Go To XYZ' button facility (the camera does not have to be synced for use of the 'Go To XYZ' facility, only the stage).

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">Coordinates sync (Homing sync)</span>
Syncing to coordinates is an optional procedure but it may be very useful to do for the most accurate performance of the 'Go To XYZ' facility.
You can instigate a coordinates sync (also called a homing sync) by ensuring you have made  tile selection prior to clicking on the 'Sync. Stage' button. Conversely, if you do not want to perform a homing sync then make sure you have no tile selected (click the 'Clear selected' button) before you click the 'Sync. Stage' button.
Due to inevitable mechanical and electronic noise errors in the stage mechnism and limit switch, etc., even if you use the exact same stage, the actual precise position of the X stage achieved with, say, 105769 atomic mictosteps after homing X to limit switch 1 on one occasion, will not be exactly the same as the actual precise position of the X stage achieved with 105769 atomic microsteps achieved after homing to the same limit switch on another occasions. The same can be said for the Y and Z axes.
Thus, simply homing the stage to the same limits as were used for the actual scan (which is the bare minimum requirement for syncing the stage) is likely to still result in some positional error when you attempt to go to a specific coordinate position for an image tile as recorded in the scan file.
To compensate for this and make re-positioning to the same coordinates as accurate as possible, even on a different day let alone a different homing session, the option to do a homing sync / coordinate sync is provided.
If you perform a coordinate sync, by ensuring a tile is selected when you click the 'Sync. Stage' button, the program will note the current coordinate position of the stage and compare those coordinates to the recorded X, Y and Z coordinates of the image tile that is currently selected. If any of these coordinates differ pardclient will send a command to the server to update the current stepcount of the relevant motor to be equal to the coordinate recorded in the scan file - for each axis. So, by the end, it will be guaranteed that he stage axes motor step counts will precisely match the step counts (coordinates) recorded in the scan file for the currently selected tile. That's it.
In order for this to be useful as a syncing procedure, it is up to you to first, manually move the stage such that the current live camera field of view matches, as perfectly as you can get it, the full image of the selected tile (and you must be using the same camera with the same resolution settings as the camera used to capture the scan - or at least a camera with a live field of view that matches the camera used to make the scan). So, by ensuring the current live view image is the same (field) as the currently selected tile and then clicking 'Sync. Stage' you will guarantee that the coordinates recorded for that tile are now also the coordinates of the actual axis motors of your live stage. It will be as if the axes were 'perfectly' homed to the exact same position as they were when you made the scan.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151">Go To XYZ</span>
You must be logged in and have an XYZ stage to use this. With the stage synced to the currently loaded scan, if you select any tile in the preview and click the 'Go To XYZ' button the stage will be instructed to go to the coordinates of the currently selected tile.
This gives you a very powerful ability to examine the specimen at specific locations having been guided by what you see in the scan images. It may be that you need to examine the area with a different set of focus planes or different optical modality (like fluorescence or phase contrast) to what was used for the scan. Or it may be that you want to look down the ocular of the microscope to see the 'live' optical image to confirm something you have seen on the scan (like the presence of an AAFB to confirm TB diagnosis after seeing 'possibly' positive images on the scan that are not, in the digital recorded image, entirely clear-cut). Whatever your reasons, this Go To facility enhances your ability to examine the specimen that has been scanned.


<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="xx-large" foreground="#1a5fb4">Co-ord. Clipboard</span>

The Co-ord. Clipboard control page is a convenience faculty to allow you to copy coordinates from one part of the GUI into entry boxes in other parts of the GUI. Refer to figure 13.1 for the rest of this chapter. The working principles are as follows:
This only copies or pastes floating point numbers or integers. It is not a general text copy/paste facility.
This will copy or paste up to 3 such numbers in one go.
Pressing one of the 'Copy' buttons will copy the numbers from a specific set of text entry boxes or an entry box into the 'Clipboard current text' entry box in this control page (shown in figure 13.1).
Pressing the 'Paste to Source' button will paste the numbers in the 'Clipboard current text' entry box into the 'Copy/Paste Source' entry box(es). How those source entry boxes are selected will be explained in detail in the rest of this chapter.

[IMAGE:image30.png]

Figure 13.1 The Co-ord. Clipboard control page. Whatever 3 numbers are in the 'Clipboard current text' entry box will be copied into the 1, 2 or 3 coordinate entry boxes that are currently marked as being the 'Copy/Paste Source'. Se text for details.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151">The 'Copy/Paste Source'</span>
The entry box or boxes that act as the source of numbers to copy from (when you press the 'Copy from Source' button) or paste into are called the 'Copy/Paste Source' entry box (or boxes). The source is selected according to the control page currently active and by which edit box user input focus is currently in. The rules will be explained below but you do not need to 'guess' as to what is currently the source because every time the source changes a message will be printed in the server console to let you know exactly what is the 'Copy/Pates Source'. For example here is a series of sequential printouts in the server console that were printed as a result of moving tabs and focus into various entry boxes:
<b>Copy/Paste Source</b><b>: Manual Commands XYZ</b>
<b>Copy/Paste Source: Manual Commands Z-Only.</b>
<b>Copy/Paste Source: XYZ-Scan Start XYZ.</b>
<b>Copy/Paste Source: Manual Commands XYZ</b>
Also, the currently selected copy/paste source is displayed directly under the 'Paste to Source' button as shown in figure 13.1. Thus there can be no doubt where you are sending the text to when you click it.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">How the Copy/Paste Source is chosen</span>
The rules for selecting what / which entry box(es) constitute the current 'Copy/Paste Source' are as follows:
When the 'Manual' control page is selected the source will be the entry boxes marked as for X, Y and Z as long as the 'Z' box does not have focus. If the 'Z' box has input focus then the source will ONLY be the Z entry box - the X and Y boxes will be ignored in any copy/paste operation in that case. Note that, according to the dynamic nature of the GUI in the Manual controls page, all 3 of those entry boxes may not be displayed and if any of them are displayed they may not be labelled as X, Y or Z. Nevertheless, the copy/paste action will pertain to whichever are showing at the time no matter what they are labelled as.
If the XYZ Scan control page is selected the source will, by default, be the 'Start at' X, Y and Z entry boxes unless you have set input focus to either of the 'Finish at' X and Y entry boxes (in which case they will become the source). If you deliberately set focus to either the X or Y 'Start at' entry boxes then the full set of three (X, Y and Z) 'Start at' entry boxes will be the source but if you set input focus in the 'Start at' Z entry box this this Z box alone will be the source - the X and Y boxes will be ignored in any copy/paste operation in that case.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151"></span>

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151">Alternative sources to copy from</span>
While the 'Copy/Paste Source' boxes are the only boxes that can be pasted to, there are two more 'read-only' sources where coordinates may be copied from and into the 'Clipboard current text' box ready to be pasted. These alternative sources are:
The X, Y and Z entry boxes next to the 'Go To XYZ' button in the Scan View control page
The values shown in the Coordinates bar for the current live XYZ stage (not the values shown in the coordinates bar when viewing a scan in the Scan View control page). Note that it is the values displayed in this bar that are copied. If the coordinates bar values have not been updated or synced to the stage then these values may not represent the actual current stage position. You can address that by issuing the manual command 'get_axes' to update these coordinates bar labels and sync them to the latest stage coordinates prior to pressing the 'Copy from CurrPos' button.
The system copy source: The  'Clipboard current text' box is editable so you can use your native OS clipboard to copy from any (e.g.) text file and paste directly into that text box - or you can manually enter values into that box via the keyboard. If using any of these generic input method just be sure to only use numbers and only a maximum of 3 numbers otherwise an error popup will be thrown if you do not conform to these rules and then try to press the 'Paste to Source' button.
You copy from these sources to the 'Clipboard current text' box by clicking the dedicated 'Copy' buttons for them (shown in figure 13.1). 






<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="xx-large" foreground="#1a5fb4">Help</span>

The Help control page provides you with tools to load, read and search custom help files as well as providing the usual 'About' and software licence information.

[IMAGE:image31.png]

Figure 14.1 The Help control page showing the About box when the 'About / License' button is pressed. However, this page can also load and display searchable custom help documents. See text for details.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151">Load and search a custom file</span>
Simply drag-drop your help file onto the text area or click the 'Load Help File' button to bring up a file chooser to select a file to load. You may load files in plain text format or Pango markup format. The file chooser will offer these in the form of 'Plain text files' (.txt files) and 'Pango markup files' (.pango files).
Once a file is loaded you may search the text by entering your search term in the entry box provided and hit 'Return' to search forwards or 'Shift+Return' to search backwards.
Note: Searching for terms that bridge mixed-formatting styles (e.g. searching for 'bold text' when formatting applies only to the word 'bold') may cause highlighting anomalies.
The search is not case sensitive.
An example of a rendered help file is shown in figure 14.2. The text is word-wrapped (so there is no horizontal scrollbar control). You can expand the viewable area vertically using the draggable transition line controls discussed on p.23 and this expanded view is shown in figure 14.2. You can also enlarge the viewable area by re-sizing the main window. See below for how to scale the font size.

[IMAGE:image32.png]

Figure 14.2 This shows a help file loaded (the PCS programming language reference manual in .pango format). Note that the view area size has been expanded for ease of viewing. You can also make the font sizes bigger or smaller using the control on the lower left labelled 'Text size (points)' currently set at 10. 

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151"></span>

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151">The Pango format</span>
In order for it to render correctly, any file you write with a .pango extension must follow the rules of the Pango markup language, alongside special image tags.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">Basic Text Formatting</span>
Use standard tags to style your text. Every opening tag must have a matching closing tag.
&lt;b&gt;Bold Text&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;i&gt;Italic Text&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;u&gt;Underlined Text&lt;/u&gt;
&lt;s&gt;Strikethrough Text&lt;/s&gt;

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">Unicode characters</span>
Unicode characters can be typed directly between the tags exactly like standard ASCII text. However, you must follow these rules:
<b>1. </b><b>File Encoding</b>: The file must be saved with UTF-8 encoding (not UTF-16, UTF-32, or Windows-1252/ANSI).
<b>2. </b><b>Direct Typing</b>: Users can mix languages freely. For example, this is completely valid Pango Markup:
Text can be mixed: &lt;b&gt;日本語&lt;/b&gt;, &lt;i&gt;العربية&lt;/i&gt;, and 🚀!
<b>3. </b><b>Escaping is Only for Syntax</b>: You only need to escape the three XML structural characters (&amp;amp; for &amp;, &amp;lt; for &lt;, &amp;gt; for &gt;). You do not need to use complicated hex codes for non-Latin alphabets or emojis.

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">Advanced Styling (&lt;span&gt;)</span>
Use the &lt;span&gt; tag with attributes to change colors, sizes, and fonts:
<b>Colors:</b>
&lt;span foreground="blue"&gt;Blue Text&lt;/span&gt;
or 
&lt;span foreground="#FF5733"&gt;Hex Color&lt;/span&gt;
<b>Size: </b>
&lt;span size="x-large"&gt;Big Text&lt;/span&gt;
or
&lt;span size="14000"&gt;14pt Text (points multiplied by 1024)&lt;/span&gt;
<b>Font:</b>
&lt;span font_family="Monospace"&gt;Fixed width text&lt;/span&gt;

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">Special Character Escaping (Mandatory)</span>
Because the file uses an XML-like syntax, you cannot use the characters &lt;, &gt;, or &amp; directly in your text. You must escape them:
Change &amp; to &amp;amp;
Change &lt; to &amp;lt;
Change &gt; to &amp;gt;

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="large" foreground="#333333">Inserting Images</span>
To insert an image, place its absolute or relative path on a standalone line using this syntax (but using directory separators that are appropriate to your OS):
[IMAGE:/path/to/image.png]

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151">The plain text format</span>
Files ending in .txt will be rendered as plain text.
Plain text documents may use UTF-8 Unicode characters directly. There is no need to use escape sequences. The file must be saved with UTF-8 encoding (not UTF-16, UTF-32, or Windows-1252/ANSI).

<span font="sans" weight="bold" size="x-large" foreground="#f66151">Font size</span>
You may scale displayed text using the 'Text size (points)' control shown at the bottom of the Help page controls in figure 14.1

[End of Document]
